WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.680
 Father Holtschneider and Dean Ganim realized that we at DePaul needed to fortify our students

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.720
 as they prepared to walk into newsrooms across the country.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.220
 And so this center was born.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.680
 And Don Mosley and I are grateful to say we were asked to direct it.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.520
 Dean Ganim and Father Holtschneider had several missions in mind.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.740
 First, to take a select number of seniors and graduate students

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.760
 who, as we like to say, are right there on the bridge,

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.620
 just about to cross over into the profession

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.540
 and give them as much grounding as we could

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.280
 in the ethical challenges that they are likely to meet,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.640
 into the complexity of stories that they will confront,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.280
 and the responsibility that they have,

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.300
 not just to their viewers or their readers or their listeners,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.700
 but to themselves to do proud work.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:08.600
 When Lester Holt and Ben Welsh, our two remarkable awardees, arrived on campus this morning,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:16.160
 it was the center's students, nine of them, who researched and wrote the interview that I moderated.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.280
 An interview, our students know, is not just a list of questions.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:29.160
 It's a piece of whole cloth with the beginning and a middle and an end and the research to back it up.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:35.200
 In class, we do deep dives into stories using that greatest of tools,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.060
 the Freedom of Information Act,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:42.860
 which is more than writing a letter for public documents used well.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.720
 It really is a comparative study of everything from the patterns of government

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.340
 to the public officials themselves.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:59.120
 One of the stories just completed is being teed up for broadcast on WTTW Chicago tonight.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.920
 And our thanks to executive producer Mary Field for being our receptive guide

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.220
 and managing editor on all of this.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.340
 If we see journalism under attack today, we at T'Pol have the antidote.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:26.660
 We call it more journalism and more of the best kind of journalism.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:34.620
 You know that you all are here today fortifies us in so many ways.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:40.020
 In this room are so many colleagues from print and broadcast and radio,

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.560
 from the producing side and the reporting side and the anchoring side,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:46.980
 the editing side.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.260
 And then there is the faculty.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.820
 We have such a great faculty,

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:57.520
 many of whom were working journalists and still are working journalists.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.740
 And so this combination is dynamite for our students.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.860
 If you would now allow me to introduce Don Mosley,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.380
 Don and I have long been a reporter-producer team.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.760
 We now carry our professional work into T'Pol as a team directing the center.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:18.920
 Don Mosley.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.800
 And now, Ben Welsh.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:31.540
 In the fall of 2003, Ben walked through the doors of what was then the DePaul Documentary Project.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.220
 He was the first of more than 50 juniors and seniors who would go on to intern with us

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:43.180
 and work with us on three documentaries and for countless, countless stories that aired on NBC5.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.140
 I'd like to say that Ben walked into our Lincoln Park office in the Schmidt Academic Center

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.420
 as a polished, aspiring young journalist.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.860
 With roots reaching back to the Amalna colony in Iowa,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:04.200
 the student who would go on to become the data editor at the Los Angeles Times was pretty geeky.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:10.340
 But what he lacked in sophistication in those days was overshadowed by an intellectual curiosity

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.140
 that was evident to Carroll and to me very early on.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.320
 His interests ranged from music to sports to politics.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.500
 After receiving his undergraduate degree here at DePaul, he received his master's from the University of Missouri.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:29.480
 His work covering the Missouri legislature helped him secure a position at the Center for Public Integrity,

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.300
 which led to being hired at the Los Angeles Times.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:33.860
 And how's that gone?

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:41.180
 Ben was part of the Pulitzer Prize winning team for their work on the 2015 San Bernardino Massacre.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:46.240
 But even before that, his very first national award was from Scripps Howard.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.940
 And here's a remarkable thing that he did.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.260
 We walked into the office at Schmidt Academic Center one day, and there was a package.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.140
 And in the package was the award that Ben won.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:03.800
 And he sent it to us so we could share it with our students so they could see what the possibilities are

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.980
 by going through the journalism program at DePaul.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:11.280
 I thought it was a remarkable and wonderful gesture for Ben.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.640
 We could go on and on about him, but I want to close with the words of Megan Garvey,

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:20.320
 who is the deputy managing editor of the Los Angeles Times.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:27.440
 It would be hard to overstate the importance of Ben's work to change how journalism is done at the L.A. Times.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.780
 He's innovative, curious, ambitious, tireless.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.640
 Sometimes he's cranky, but he usually reserves that just for me.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:36.620
 She goes on to write,

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.820
 I met Ben nine years ago when he first came to the Times.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.840
 We worked together on California's War Dead Database, our first big public database project.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:53.520
 Now my job is to reimagine how quality journalism gets created, distributed, and stays viable.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.140
 If I had never met Ben, I wouldn't be doing what I am today.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.520
 That's a story many others I know could also tell.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.480
 Beyond his value as a newsroom colleague, Ben has devoted countless hours outside of his day job

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:11.740
 to teaching and expanding the role of data journalist in the larger community.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.740
 He is a fierce advocate for keeping public records open.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.820
 He also happens to be a genuinely decent person and excellent company.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.140
 I probably embarrassed him enough, so I'll stop now.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:28.660
 Please offer Ben the congratulations of the entire newsroom of the Los Angeles Times.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.420
 And to that, we'll add DePaul's congratulations as well.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:37.620
 You're killing me, Don.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:39.160
 You're killing me.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:41.360
 Now I've got to talk.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:42.780
 Now you're going to talk.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:47.040
 Wow.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.200
 Well, I'm getting emotional.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:51.720
 Whew.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:52.880
 No, all right.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:54.880
 Thank you, Don.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:55.680
 Thank you so much.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.560
 I'm so honored to receive this reward.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:03.180
 And I'm always happy to have a chance to come back to Chicago, where it's a place I really felt like I grew up.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.780
 And a lot of great things happened for me.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.040
 And I'm always eager to come back, as long as it's not winter.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:12.680
 Right?

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:22.980
 And I'm especially glad on this trip to have met the bright young students at DePaul's new Center for Journalism Integrity and Excellence.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:32.280
 And where I see a lot of the great things I want to talk about right now becoming part of DePaul as an institution and something that so many others can benefit from.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:45.120
 And on this occasion, I also recognize that there's no religious institution that's properly run, as DePaul is, that could ever responsibly invite me to deliver a commencement address.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.440
 And risk tainting their valuable young minds.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.860
 So I'm just going to take my shot right now.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:52.360
 All right?

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.160
 Brace yourselves.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.420
 I'm going to give you kids some advice.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:55.820
 All right?

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:56.580
 You're back there, right?

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:57.800
 All right.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:04.840
 And like all fables force-fed to the young by their elders, this one starts with a boring piece of autobiography.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.220
 Sorry, that's how it goes.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.380
 You'll understand one day.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:08.780
 All right?

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:09.460
 You guys ready?

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:10.880
 All right.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.340
 Here comes your advice from Uncle Ben.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.040
 All right?

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:17.140
 So my story starts about 15 years ago on the Lincoln Park campus.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.740
 Don already told a good piece of it.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:26.680
 When I was working on the top floor of the Schmidt Academic Center in what was then the Department of Communication Office, where Professor Bruce Emerson worked at that time.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:32.280
 And I was earning some extra money as a student there, answering the phones and doing odd jobs around the office.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:42.400
 And one of the many important responsibilities that I was trusted with was putting up flyers on the Department Bulletin Board of new things happening around campus.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:53.900
 And one day it just so happened that a new posting said there was an opening for an intern to work with two journalists who were taking up residence at DePaul, Carroll and Don.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:09:00.640
 And, oh, by the way, their new office was only about three fours below where I was standing, and I just need to take the elevator down.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:05.140
 And as I was pinning that notice to the board, a thought popped into my head.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:11.700
 I don't really know what this job is, but it's probably a hell of a lot more interesting than pinning flyers on a bulletin board.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:20.440
 And so I jumped in the elevator, and because I was the first one to see the listing, I think I was probably the first one to apply.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.500
 And somehow I lucked into that internship.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:27.660
 And I didn't know it at the time, but it was probably one of the luckiest days of my life.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:34.080
 And I soon started, and I had no idea what I was doing.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.040
 I'll just confess now.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.640
 But I quickly understood that the luckiest thing for me was mainly just to see Carroll and Don work.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:50.640
 I was inspired by their sense of purpose, by their dedication to finding and telling stories that matter, stories that otherwise wouldn't be told or told to the standards they expected.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:59.240
 And once I got past the fear that I would screw everything up and they'd fire me, which, let's be honest, was pretty well-founded, I think, at that time,

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.080
 I started to wonder if I could ever take a stab myself.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:10.320
 And I got my chance one day when Carroll and Don received a tip that a local law firm was milking money from a Chicago suburb,

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.900
 you know the ones, by overbilling the city for legal work.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.820
 And it sounded like a good story of local corruption.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:23.720
 And even me, even I, knew that this might be the kind of thing that should be on the news, right?

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:25.340
 But where to begin?

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:31.760
 If it was true, there would be evidence in the city's financial books, which are public records, Don told me.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:39.380
 And then he challenged me to sit down and write out a public records request, something I'd never done before, so that we could get to the bottom of it.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:49.500
 And then, for weeks, he egged me on, again and again, when a shady city official tried to delay, skirt the law, and discourage me from pursuing this request.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:53.780
 But because Don kept pushing me, I kept pushing the city.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:55.760
 Weeks went by.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.240
 A lot of sternly written letters went back and forth.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.240
 But then one day, a package arrived in the mail.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:03.720
 Inside was the data we'd asked for.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:05.880
 There was only one problem.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:18.680
 The city had sent it as a massive printout of paper, a scroll over 10, 20 feet in length that was connected by those old, you guys remember the 1980s printers with the holes and the tear sheets?

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.900
 The kids won't remember this, but the kids won't remember this, so we'll tell them about it afterwards.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:33.480
 And there was no total cost for what these legal fees were, and the papers were kind of in inconsistent order and in an odd format, and sprinkled throughout it kind of helter-skelter.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:43.200
 And to make sense of it all, we would need to puzzle through this nasty scroll page by page and add up all those numbers, which, you know, was tedious, unglamorous work.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:49.980
 You know, not the kind of thing you imagine, you know, important journalists on television spending their time doing, right?

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:51.380
 Wrong.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:12:00.440
 With Carol's and Don's encouragement, I built my first spreadsheet in Microsoft Excel, hammered in all the figures line by line, and hours later after I was done,

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:05.800
 we had enough support for a story that exposed these exorbitant legal fees being charged to the city.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:15.860
 Carol read the big numbers I had added up on camera and even dropped the hilarious long piece of paper from the top of a ladder that she climbed to illustrate their size.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:29.780
 And it was for me, like, that was a moment for me when it clicked, when I saw and I understood and I had learned that all the slow, frustrating work that I had put in made sense.

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.520
 It wasn't just worthwhile, it was essential.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:44.860
 I learned that what separates reporters who move the needle, the ones who get the story, the Carol's and the Don's of the worlds, the Lester Holt's of the worlds, from the ones who don't, isn't all that complicated.

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:46.500
 It's hard work.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:47.880
 It's persistence.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:52.540
 It's a willingness to take on tedious tasks others haven't or won't.

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:55.640
 And most of all it's a determination to finish the job.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:03.040
 And despite all the changes I've seen in our industry since I got started way back in SAC, that truth is still standing today.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:08.700
 It's true whether you work on a traditional old school city corruption story like that.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:15.640
 It's true if you're experimenting with new story forms online to try to reach an audience that we've lost.

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:22.560
 It's true if you're doing something weird like me and trying to become a computer programmer and still be a journalist at the same time.

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:28.840
 If you do the work and you do it the right way, like Carol and Don can teach you guys, you too can move that needle.

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:33.440
 But if that's something you want to do, that's something you really want to do, you've got to start now.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:46.100
 And you've got to start by picking up that phone, getting outside your comfort zone, filing that public records request, not giving up, digging into that ugly database, asking questions that haven't been asked before, and banging on those doors.

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:54.100
 And when whatever happens next, after you start off on that, frustrates you, you have to keep trying, because you're probably on to something, right?

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:56.300
 And it's only by pushing that you're ever going to get there.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:02.320
 And the really good stories, the ones that are worth telling, they don't come easy, you've got to nail them down.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:12.040
 And the best kept secret of all, the thing I think we ought to advertise, is that when you're on the trail of something good like that, there's no better feeling.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.620
 Or at least not one we can talk about in front of Father Holschneider.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:28.680
 Carol once told me, it's like breathing different air when we were out on assignment, and she could tell I was feeling it.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:30.060
 And she was right.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:32.380
 And as soon as I got my taste, I was hooked.

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:36.000
 But like any good journalist, you shouldn't take my word for it.

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:37.700
 You should go check it out for yourself.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.040
 You won't be disappointed.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:39.940
 Thank you.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:51.640
 There was never any question about who we hoped would be our distinguished journalist awardee, our very first.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:53.820
 It was always Lester Holt.

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:59.800
 It was a challenging time for NBC when Lester Holt became its principal anchor.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:08.240
 And it's a challenging time for all of us in news, in this polarized political climate in which we live.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:12.920
 Integrity has never been more critical.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.020
 Excellence has never been more important.

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:28.040
 Journalism is lifted up by the elegance, the high ethical standards, and the excellence of Lester Holt.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:37.080
 Our students are inspired by his example, and we watched it this morning as they met with Lester and Ben.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:46.340
 And so today, DePaul University and our center thanks Lester Holt for making a difference

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:50.920
 and is so proud to give you our distinguished journalist award.

00:15:50.920 --> 00:16:09.880
 Thank you, thank you everyone, and thanks to DePaul and the Center for Journalism Integrity.

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:11.260
 Congratulations, Ben.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:18.980
 Carol, it means a lot getting this award, but certainly from you, someone who certainly raised the bar of investigative journalism in this town.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:26.500
 You know, I should tell you, walking over with Carol from DePaul is an interesting experience.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:33.360
 When you go by the federal building, she gives a running dialogue of the various governors and members of Congress

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.540
 and people who have had perp walks in and out of that building.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:37.300
 It was fascinating.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:40.980
 Next time we'll do it in a double-decker bus, and we'll charge people.

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:46.040
 But it was nothing like taking a stroll through downtown with a hard-hitting investigative journalist.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:49.420
 But it is great to be back in Chicago.

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:49.860
 It's great.

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.120
 It's old home week for me.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:58.380
 A lot of people I worked a lot of years with and adore in this room, and it's very special to be here.

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:01.600
 When I came to Chicago, it was 1986.

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:09.520
 Some of you may remember the station I was working at was the target of a boycott by Operation Push.

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:14.180
 I was kind of brought in, not quite knowing what I was walking into.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:21.460
 It was a stressful time for a 27-year-old, but Chicago embraced me, and I embraced Chicago back.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:32.480
 And I have such incredible great memories of being here, not only because it was then and still is arguably the best news town in America,

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:39.360
 but because it set the course for who I would become as a broadcast journalist.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:44.920
 It was here that I learned that being a reporter is not a career step.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.220
 Being a reporter is what we are.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:55.760
 It's the fundamental essence of all of us who report, who are journalists, whether that title is anchor or whatever it is, that your main title is a reporter.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:18:03.080
 It was here that I saw examples of anchors who, you know, not only read the news, but they led their newsrooms.

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:13.320
 And it's a lesson that was not lost on me and one that I take with me every day, that lesson of how to lead and what being an anchor is all about.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.100
 So I left here 17 years ago.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:19.040
 A lot of people didn't know up until about a month before I left.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:21.100
 I wasn't sure where I was going to go.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:24.700
 I was in the middle of a transition, as they say, in this business.

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:37.300
 I took the leap into the world of cable news at MSNBC at what I didn't know then would turn out to be the start of about a four to five-year news cycle like none we've ever seen.

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:44.480
 I arrived in time to cover the 2000 presidential campaign, the subsequent Florida recount drama.

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:53.100
 That was followed by the 9-11 attacks, the war in Afghanistan, and later the run-up to the invasion of Iraq and the invasion itself.

00:18:53.640 --> 00:19:01.180
 Some of those moments along the way were moments where people were literally glued to their television, well, not literally, but they were glued to their television sets.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:02.920
 You know, life stopped.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:07.060
 People sat to watch what was happening, and they were clinging to our words.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.600
 The future and the security of the nation were at stake.

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:16.720
 And to some extent, our national values were tested and challenged as we became a post-9-11 nation.

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:19.860
 It was an amazing time to be a journalist.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:30.340
 And in this current age of a whole new set of challenges, many of which are byproducts of that era of terror and war, it still is an amazing time to be a journalist.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:38.520
 And anyone who doubts the future or commitment to a healthy press needs to only look around this room for reassurance.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:43.160
 Ours is still a high calling, and it still has a high purpose.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:53.340
 At virtually every place that I'm invited to speak or make comments these days, the first topic of interest is the state of journalism, particularly under the age of Trump.

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:56.020
 So let me offer my two cents on that.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:05.020
 It is unfortunate, and it's troubling to hear this or any other leader disparage and malign the media, not because it hurts my feelings,

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:17.840
 but because if someone in power can successfully discredit an institution that society depends on most to hold our leaders and institutions accountable, then there is no one.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:22.300
 And I simply ask, what then, if there's no one in that position?

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:34.020
 The good news is, in the case of organizations like NBC News, you can't simply tweet away or insult away decades of integrity and excellence and a commitment to truth and accuracy.

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:44.420
 Our future, I'm pretty sure, is bright, and I suspect we'll be hard at work covering the administration after this, the one after that, and my contract ends at that point.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:50.420
 But there will be other people doing it, I'm pretty sure.

00:20:51.460 --> 00:21:02.000
 At nightly news, our role, in part, is to provide a safe harbor for excellent journalism in the stormy sea of rapid-fire information that we live in,

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:06.840
 where facts and fiction, truth and rumor, it seems, are randomly tossed about.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:11.440
 I, for one, proudly embrace the label of mainstream media.

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:23.100
 To me, it represents a commitment to the time-honored practices of digging for facts, shining the light in dark corners, giving voice to the voiceless, holding our leaders and institutions accountable,

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:29.480
 and attributing and being transparent of where our information comes from.

00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:36.360
 Our tagline at nightly news is, understanding matters, and it does, more than ever.

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:43.860
 The issues are complicated, the solutions are not black and white, and the spin and red herrings are relentless.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:52.180
 It requires all of our efforts, and some effort by the viewer, to embrace critical thinking on a whole new level.

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:58.120
 You're no doubt familiar with the old journalism adage, if your mother tells you she loves you, check it out.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:02.820
 Well, these days, that adage applies to consumers of news as well.

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:04.660
 We have to ask, what's the source?

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:06.760
 Who benefits from the information?

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.980
 And does it stand up to the facts?

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:12.980
 We cannot afford to be lazy consumers of news anymore.

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:16.760
 Certainly, we can't become lazy reporters of news either.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:20.380
 So, yes, this is a good time to do what we do.

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.100
 It's a great time to be a journalist.

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:29.600
 I just want to give a big nod to DePaul and the Center for Journalism Integrity for ensuring the legacy of this noble profession.

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:35.540
 Now, I kept my remarks short because I actually prefer to answer questions, which is what I'd like to do.

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:37.700
 I think we have time for a few questions.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.180
 I'll work around with the microphone, so no worries.

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:42.560
 We have students who are in the microphone.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:51.040
 Okay, but while we get prepared for questions, let me just tell you that President Trump, and I know, it was a slip.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.920
 It's like writing your checks in January, you still write the wrong year, it's the same thing.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:00.420
 The President Trump and the Prime Minister of Italy are holding a news conference today.

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:01.700
 What does that mean to you and me?

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:08.220
 I'm anchoring a special report that we're offering to the affiliates at 2.45 Central Time.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.420
 So that's my way of saying, that sounded really pretentious, didn't it?

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:17.720
 Yes, I'm doing a news conference with the President, but I actually am, and so I've got to go to NBC Tower.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:19.420
 So when I'm done here, I'm going to slip out.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.000
 I don't mean to be rude, but I just wanted to throw that out.

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:24.360
 But do we have anybody who wants to ask a question?

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:30.120
 Okay, goodbye, thank you.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:32.260
 Try the veal.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:34.560
 Oh, yeah, please, yeah.

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:36.640
 I'll come to you.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:38.060
 Oh, no, we've got a microphone coming to you.

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:39.040
 Okay, all right.

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:40.820
 You don't need a microphone.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:41.240
 Oh, thanks.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:48.940
 So tell us, what's the big difference between it being local news and the network news?

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:53.100
 I mean, what have you seen over the years as far as the challenges?

00:23:54.420 --> 00:24:01.080
 Obviously, you know, bigger platform, but what, from your Chicago experience, have you carried over to what you do every day?

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:04.420
 Well, I mean, when I was doing local news, I think we tried to be all things.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:13.480
 But I think what you're seeing in local news in general, and I think you would agree, is that there's more focus on local, on neighborhoods, on what's really happening right in front of us.

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:18.720
 The big change for me going to, on the national level, was suddenly you consume news in a different way.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:26.480
 You know, I obviously followed all the news, but now everything is relevant, so your radar is always spinning, and you hear something, you know, what did they say about Iran today?

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:27.840
 What happened about North Korea today?

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:29.520
 And how it's going to affect your life.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:40.760
 I was in a booking meeting the other day discussing which world leaders we would like to try and book for me to interview, and it's like, how cool, what a job, a cool job that is, right?

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:44.780
 But I think that there's more of a delineation in a really good way.

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:54.860
 Local stations now are becoming hyper-local, and we are, you know, picking up and, you know, looking at the world through, you know, a different lens, a broader lens.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.060
 It's tough doing a broadcast like mine.

00:24:58.060 --> 00:25:08.640
 It's only 22 minutes long, and you've got to kind of curate the day and, oh, by the way, highlight those things that distinguish us from our competitors and our hard-hitting investigations and those sorts of things.

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:12.540
 So it's a challenge every day trying to make it all fit.

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:17.220
 Yes, got a question back there?

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:19.360
 We'll get the microphone to you.

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:27.940
 I'm curious, how did the values that you learned as a child follow you into the professional world?

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:33.140
 How did the values you learned as a child follow you into the professional world?

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:41.720
 Well, you know, I think, you know, I inherited from my parents a pretty level head, which is important.

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:44.760
 You know, I like an intelligent discussion.

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:48.420
 And I think that as a journalist, that's what I try to foster.

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:51.420
 I don't like screaming, people screaming past each other.

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:54.360
 I like people who really provoke good thought and good discussion.

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:55.380
 That came from my values.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:58.400
 Also, compassion was a big part of my growing up.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:04.020
 And I, this is one of my pet projects, where we need to bring more compassion into journalism.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:09.700
 Remembering that a lot of the people we cover didn't ask to be in the news.

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:13.620
 They're in the news because of circumstances that, you know, they were a victim of circumstances.

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:17.840
 And we march into their lives sometimes, sometimes at their lowest moments.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:25.880
 And I always try to remember that could be me, that could be my mother, that could be my father, you know, somebody in my family in these circumstances.

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:28.220
 How would I want them to be treated?

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:30.620
 How would I want my story told?

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:37.260
 And so I think respect and compassion are really important parts of what we do.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:39.340
 Mr. Menno here.

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:40.600
 Yes.

00:26:41.140 --> 00:26:41.540
 Mr. Menno.

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:52.240
 Because I'm involved with the presidential debates, would you tell us about your experience in moderating the first debate last year?

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:53.940
 You see the sweat rolling down my head?

00:26:55.560 --> 00:27:00.300
 I still have post-traumatic syndrome from the debate.

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:06.140
 I will tell you, that was the single hardest thing I've ever done in my career.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.240
 And I think it would be hard to top it in terms of difficulty.

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:17.960
 You know, when I was asked, I mean, it still was an incredible honor to do the first debate in an election cycle like that.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:24.160
 But I have to tell you, in the couple of weeks leading up to it, it was bizarre turning on cable news

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:31.600
 and hearing people talk about me and what I should ask and what I might do and what bias I might have.

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:35.180
 And I'm screaming back at the TV, hey, you know, and no one's listening to me, of course.

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:40.160
 But, you know, both campaigns were, to some extent, their surrogates were playing me.

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:41.900
 You know, he's going to be hard.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:43.800
 He's not going to have a fact check and all this.

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:47.800
 And I would like to tell you, you let that stuff roll off, but it gets in your head.

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:54.040
 And so now you're sitting there with your back to 100 million people and these two candidates.

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:56.580
 And all I wanted to do was say, Mommy.

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:09.460
 It was a difficult format, and I have to tell you, I was knocked on my heels.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.060
 I mean, they came out, and it was like a heavyweight fight.

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.960
 Oh, my God, everybody slow down, and I'm trying to go through my questions.

00:28:15.500 --> 00:28:19.680
 And you hit a point of calm, and it's like, you know what, the heck with this format.

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:21.000
 I'm just going to be a journalist.

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:23.440
 I'm going to ask the unanswered questions.

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:25.860
 And that got me in trouble a little bit with some folks.

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:27.340
 But that's, you know, that's all I know.

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:32.220
 The important thing for people to remember is being a journalist does not make you a moderator.

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:35.960
 That's kind of a unique skill, skill set.

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:38.400
 So I just, you know, did the best I could.

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:42.820
 It was the next several days reading my email was quite entertaining.

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:44.520
 Great job.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:45.540
 Awesome questions.

00:28:45.860 --> 00:28:46.220
 Loser.

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:46.940
 Pathetic.

00:28:47.240 --> 00:28:50.260
 It was, you know, it was something for everybody.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:52.840
 But thank you for putting me in that position.

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:53.800
 I appreciate that.

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:59.660
 No, it was an amazing, amazing experience.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.080
 And I have not watched it yet.

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:03.140
 I keep telling myself.

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:05.260
 I still have all my notes and all my questions.

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:11.140
 The story someday will be told of our preparation for that debate, which was quite extensive.

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:14.080
 It involved a lot of people, a lot of role-playing.

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:21.240
 And without telling you who was doing the role-playing, there were a couple of moments that went exactly as we rehearsed them.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:22.340
 I almost stopped them.

00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:25.420
 I said, I want you to, this person does you so great.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:29.400
 It was, there were a few moments that, I mean, almost word for word.

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:31.280
 So I had a great team.

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:35.720
 But, yeah, I'll still be getting treatment and counseling for that for some time to come.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:37.780
 But I think I had time for a few more questions.

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:38.780
 Hi.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:40.140
 Sure.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:42.120
 My name is Roger Crockett.

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:48.660
 And for many years, I was a writer and editor for Business Week magazine here in Chicago.

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:55.400
 And I mention that because back in 2010, McGraw-Hill, who owned Business Week, sold the magazine.

00:29:55.400 --> 00:30:00.560
 It had lost profitability and it's now become Bloomberg Business Week.

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:06.720
 But it demonstrates the changes and the challenges that have faced the world of publishing and journalism.

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:18.020
 And so in this age of Twitter where the competitive forces against journalists are so much greater than they were when some of us were coming up in the field,

00:30:18.020 --> 00:30:28.740
 can you talk a bit about, you know, for the new generation of journalists, how they really forge their ways through this very challenging competitive field?

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:30.560
 Yeah, I mean, there are two worlds that we live in.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:37.340
 The one is that, you know, as much as we focus on news and reporting, we're not blind to the fact that we're part of bigger businesses.

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:43.000
 And, you know, fortunately, NBC News has a great parent company and we're fully supported.

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:49.440
 But those wins change, especially in publishing, you know, as new ownership comes in and new thoughts and new directions.

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:56.000
 And, you know, as a reporter, you've got to, you know, obviously hang on to your integrity and your focus while weathering those changes.

00:30:56.640 --> 00:31:00.100
 But to your point about social media, the playing field is so different.

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:02.500
 Everyone wants to talk about the media, the media, the media.

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:04.080
 And I'm like, what are you talking about, the media?

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:05.580
 I mean, everyone's in the media now.

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:07.640
 Every tweet is the media.

00:31:08.060 --> 00:31:19.840
 So we have to, I think, define who we are and what we want to be in a way, maybe a more forceful way with our readers and our viewers,

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:21.360
 that this is what we do.

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:22.920
 I do mainstream media.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:26.020
 We do, you know, independent reporting.

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:29.780
 That doesn't mean what they do at another outlet is different.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:34.600
 But we've got to maybe educate our audiences a little bit about what we offer.

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:40.580
 I always remind people that I don't do my newscast to make people feel good.

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:42.580
 I hope there's some stories that may make you feel good.

00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:46.280
 But, you know, news steps on your toes sometimes.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:50.740
 News sometimes, you know, tells you, you know, I know you thought this was true, but guess what?

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:51.400
 That's not true.

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:54.860
 Sometimes you want to throw your shoe at the TV.

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:57.300
 Other times you want to go damn straight.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:00.320
 So that's the dynamic of news.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:07.740
 I think it's important to remind people because we are in a popularity contest to the extent that circulation and ratings and those sorts of things.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:15.660
 But in terms of the content that we curate and we report every day, that's not with the aim of being liked or being popular and making people want to hug us.

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:20.060
 We're not here to tell you, at least our organization, we're not here to tell people what they want to hear.

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:24.460
 And, you know, sometimes that gets mistaken for a bias.

00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:27.680
 But to me, that's what news is and what news should be.

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:30.760
 Hi.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:40.180
 Earlier today, during your interview at DePaul, you mentioned that you have biases assigned to you, such as you're an African-American, you're a male, you're a Christian.

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:47.200
 I was just wondering how you separate those biases from your work so that you can present your news objectively.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:54.440
 Yeah, and that was part of a discussion we had today, and I was describing how all of us come to life with a world view.

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:55.800
 I was born in the United States.

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:57.040
 I was raised in the United States.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:32:59.560
 I'm a Christian.

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:00.400
 I'm straight.

00:33:00.700 --> 00:33:01.700
 I'm African-American.

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:03.180
 All those things form who you are.

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:06.900
 Now, that doesn't mean that, you know, that affects every story I report.

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:12.300
 But I think it's important to know that, that we all have this view of the world.

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:20.860
 As Americans, we tend to probably give more credence to something that comes, you know, from, you know, from our leaders here than in another country.

00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:24.940
 Part of that is because that's where, you know, we're from, and that's what we know.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:30.840
 It doesn't really affect the how I report stories per se.

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:34.460
 As I said, I love a good, intelligent discussion.

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:42.300
 But it makes me know that I need other people, that we need a collaborative newsroom, and that's what we rely on.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:50.200
 When I'm done here after the special report, we'll meet, and I'll be on the conference call discussing tonight's newscast, and we'll push back at each other.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:56.240
 And a lot of that pushback is based on our differences and what, you know, we know, you know.

00:33:56.900 --> 00:34:03.520
 I'm from the West Coast originally, so sometimes I'm the guy that says, you know what, that may be a big New York story, but, you know, in the West that may not play.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:08.740
 It feeds our diversity, which makes us better at what we do.

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:11.020
 And it's not just racial and gender diversity.

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:19.040
 It's this diversity of thought and experiences, because news is a collaborative project of putting it together every night.

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:20.720
 Yes.

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:23.060
 Thank you for being here, and congratulations.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:29.160
 It's wonderful to have all the journalists in the room who will back up my recent Facebook post that we're here with thousands of people,

00:34:29.160 --> 00:34:35.120
 the biggest audience ever gathered, to see a network news anchor in the Union League Club's history.

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:36.020
 So thank you for that.

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:37.140
 It's record-setting.

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:39.240
 Do not fact-check that, man.

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:41.340
 Yeah, and everyone will confirm that, I'm sure.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:47.940
 My question is, in an era where we have the proliferation of news outlets, online, cable, broadcast,

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:56.020
 where people can quite literally select the news source that reinforces their worldview and exclude or ignore those that might challenge it,

00:34:56.020 --> 00:35:07.380
 how do we get people to go outside their comfort zone as consumers of news to look at, listen to broadcasts, read newspapers that may not coincide with their worldview?

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:08.340
 How do we do that?

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:16.560
 See, I don't know, because it's so comforting to consume stuff that says you're right, or that's the world we live in.

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:25.640
 And I don't know, that's our big challenge right now, is to help people understand that, you know, mainstream media, independent journalism is not a bad thing.

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:32.900
 It fosters an interesting conversation, and it's okay to, you know, change your minds.

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:38.220
 I think we're living in a world right now that I kind of liken it to baseball loyalties.

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:42.000
 You know, you're a Mets fan, you're a Cubs fan.

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:50.580
 You can't convince someone to switch teams, even if your team is, you know, 20 games out of first place.

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:59.300
 You stick with them, and I think there's a lot of that, and that may not be a perfect analogy, but I think that's what's going on.

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:00.520
 I don't know how to do that.

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:05.320
 What I always tell people, this question came up in another thing I was at, I said, stop talking politics.

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:07.880
 Why does everything have to be framed in politics right now?

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:17.920
 One of the things we're doing as a broadcast is trying to look beyond stories, and not everything has to be framed in, you know, Democrat or Republican,

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:20.900
 but it feels like it is, like it hovers over everything.

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:28.880
 So, you know, I just try every night to try and, you know, broaden the discourse over all the things that affect us,

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:35.940
 and, you know, we do a segment right now called Inspiring America that Carol alluded to, and it's really important.

00:36:36.080 --> 00:36:38.460
 It's not, these are not throwaway feel-good stories.

00:36:38.540 --> 00:36:43.400
 These are stories that, you know, they are about people who are inspiring us, people who are doing really good things,

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:48.520
 and sometimes at the end I'll even comment, you know, it just kind of reminds you there's a lot of good in this country.

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:57.020
 There's a lot of good out there, and I think we all need those reminders that we can have discussions about a lot of things that don't necessarily involve politics.

00:36:57.660 --> 00:36:59.680
 That's the, that's just me.

00:37:01.260 --> 00:37:02.320
 I'd probably have time for one more.

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:02.560
 Yes.

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:03.280
 One more.

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:04.080
 Hello.

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:04.540
 Sir?

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:05.660
 Over here.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:08.160
 Okay, I also know you have a question.

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:09.380
 Wait a minute.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:10.360
 Linda McLennan's going to trump you.

00:37:11.620 --> 00:37:12.840
 Who inspires me?

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:15.080
 Oh, I'll be right with you, Linda.

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:16.880
 Sorry, I'm used to it.

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:22.620
 I, you know, I, there are certain people I've covered along the way that stick out.

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:26.940
 I just remember, and a real quick story, because it's really weird.

00:37:27.240 --> 00:37:37.520
 So I'm in Afghanistan, this would have been 2012, and we're covering, we're doing a story on this Air Force Combat Rescue chopper unit.

00:37:37.520 --> 00:37:44.240
 They go pluck wounded off the battlefield, taking fire and all this, and I'm talking with these guys, and they said, they turned to one guy and said,

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:46.400
 Jimmy, show Lester, tell Lester.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:47.680
 I'm like, what are you talking about?

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:48.340
 He says, all right.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:51.040
 So we walk out to the helicopter, he says, you've got to get on your back.

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:59.560
 I'm getting on our back, and there's a hole in the bottom of the helicopter, and he had signed it, the one, the one that almost got, or almost got me with the date.

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:02.080
 And the story, and it happened the day before.

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:09.720
 They were going in to rescue some wounded, there's been a horrible firefight in this, in this canyon, and they went in, and they took fire.

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:12.560
 He gets hit and sprawls across the helicopter.

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:14.980
 They rush him back in the chopper.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:19.960
 Turns out the bullet comes up under his goggle, just pierces the edge of his helmet.

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:21.240
 He only needed five stitches.

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:26.640
 But he's talking, and I'm saying, you know, man, that's God's way of saying, get the hell out of here.

00:38:27.100 --> 00:38:30.220
 And he was really so passionate about, no, these guys need us.

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:31.680
 You know, they're on that battlefield.

00:38:31.780 --> 00:38:32.540
 We've got to get in there.

00:38:32.860 --> 00:38:33.920
 I'm going back tomorrow.

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:36.440
 And it was just an incredible story, and it always stuck with me.

00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:38.900
 And I told that story many, many times.

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:42.160
 Fast forward a month ago on Amazon.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:47.140
 I'm looking for a book written by another soldier I had met on another trip to Afghanistan.

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:49.880
 And I stumble upon Jimmy had written a book.

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:51.380
 And, by the way, I'm in the book.

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:55.740
 So, and then, this is even crazier.

00:38:55.820 --> 00:38:56.520
 So I email him.

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:58.380
 I say, hey, you know, it's great.

00:38:58.500 --> 00:38:59.160
 You wrote the book.

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:01.000
 And I'm glad you're well.

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:01.900
 I didn't know where you lived.

00:39:02.240 --> 00:39:05.540
 So I'm going to Seattle the next week to do something with our station there.

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:07.740
 He finds out and says, hey, I'm in Seattle.

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:08.760
 Can I come down and see you?

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:09.700
 And he gives me the book.

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:12.220
 And it was a nice moment.

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:13.740
 And I said to him, I said, you don't know.

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:17.400
 But I have told your story so many times because you inspired me.

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:21.460
 And there's a lot of people along the way that I have met on this job that I just think, wow.

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:22.580
 Because you know what?

00:39:23.440 --> 00:39:27.440
 Certain people, you do their stories, and you think, what would I do?

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:29.320
 And what would I be capable of?

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:32.600
 Because I do a lot of these stories, and I think, man, I'd be curled up in a ball somewhere.

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:35.060
 And yet people put one foot in front of the other.

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:36.200
 Anyway, that's a long story.

00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:40.040
 But that's my, people you don't know inspire me.

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:41.700
 Wow, that took a long time.

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:44.320
 My co-anchor, Linda McLennan.

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:49.300
 Thank you.

00:39:50.460 --> 00:39:58.580
 I hope that you know how much people feel so happy for you.

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:05.320
 Still to this day, and we were together for 14 years, 16 years, but still to this day,

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:09.840
 I'll run into people and I'll say, Lester Holt is doing such an amazing job.

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:21.320
 I hope you understand that in this, you know, the third city, the second city, that people really adore you and think that you've had, you have gotten everything that you've deserved to have had.

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:26.200
 So my question is this, what about the kids?

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:26.820
 What about the kids?

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:29.980
 Our kids are sort of the same ages.

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:35.740
 My, I hate to say it, but my kids do not watch nightly news.

00:40:36.020 --> 00:40:37.780
 They don't watch TV news.

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:40.040
 They don't listen to radio news.

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:49.800
 What are we going to do about this generation that does not tune in to the sources that we all feel are the credible sources?

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:54.480
 I'm really concerned, especially given the politics of today.

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:55.660
 What are we going to do?

00:40:55.720 --> 00:40:56.480
 It's a good question.

00:40:56.580 --> 00:41:02.140
 You know, my wife was with a friend who had a younger friend, and she said, this is Lester Holt's wife.

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:07.420
 She works at NBC, and she said, and not trying to be funny, I said, is that the fake news channel?

00:41:08.180 --> 00:41:15.980
 And, you know, it was very telling, because this is a person, I'm not making fun of her, but, I mean, she's a person who doesn't consume a lot of news.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:20.060
 And, you know, you pick up little things, and that is worrisome.

00:41:20.180 --> 00:41:25.900
 I mean, you know, in a lot of places we're seeing audiences for, you know, certain broadcasts lower.

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:32.580
 I mean, there's still 23, 24 million people watching the three evening newscasts, so we're, you know, we're not going out of business anytime soon.

00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:35.280
 But there's something about the relevance.

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:41.380
 I mean, we've all been engaged, all of us in broadcast, certainly, and print, have been engaged in this trying to find the viewer.

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:43.720
 We're, you know, our jobs haven't changed.

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:49.620
 We still report news, but the viewers are moving from TV to the back of a taxi cab to their phones.

00:41:49.840 --> 00:41:51.380
 I mean, I might be on your toaster next week.

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:51.760
 I don't know.

00:41:52.760 --> 00:41:54.720
 But, oh, I wasn't supposed to give that away, was I?

00:41:56.540 --> 00:41:58.720
 But, no, it is incredibly challenging.

00:41:59.100 --> 00:42:04.600
 And although I have to tell you, there have been some moments where I've met people who are not news viewers at all, have no idea what I do.

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:12.520
 And I think, would that be kind of blissful just to be, I mean, you know, to like, wow, not be burdened by all these things.

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:14.460
 But it's a serious question.

00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:16.040
 It deserves a serious answer.

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:18.360
 And I don't know what the answer is.

00:42:18.360 --> 00:42:25.400
 But people will only, people need to understand what we do and how important it is.

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:34.400
 And maybe it will take something, I hate to say it, something, you know, life-changing that will remind people, wow, I wish I had paid better attention.

00:42:34.680 --> 00:42:41.440
 I wish I had known that information so I could, you know, write my congressman or vote it differently or whatever.

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:53.480
 And I don't wish for that, but I think it may be something dramatic that will wake people up, that you need to find out what's going on and what our leaders are doing and hold them accountable.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:55.960
 And that's why we all stay in this good fight.

00:42:56.440 --> 00:42:57.900
 I have hijacked this afternoon.

00:42:58.020 --> 00:42:58.860
 I didn't mean to do that.

00:42:59.140 --> 00:42:59.820
 Yeah, I did, actually.

00:42:59.920 --> 00:43:03.320
 But anyway, but thank you so much for the honor, Carol.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:04.040
 I just appreciate it.

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:16.580
 As many of you know, I can't miss an opportunity to brag a little bit about the college.

00:43:16.580 --> 00:43:21.300
 So I'm going to end by telling you about the College of Communication.

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:22.960
 And it's really easy.

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:25.380
 For us, it's about the students.

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:38.700
 We encourage our students, in the spirit of intention values, to develop their capabilities in order to serve others, enriching their surroundings, their community, and the world at large.

00:43:38.700 --> 00:43:47.700
 So the bar is high, and our honorees today are a great example of where our students will be one day.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.040
 And many of our students are on their way.

00:43:51.380 --> 00:44:00.660
 The number of regional and national awards that they have garnered indicates that they're definitely going to be reaching that point as well.

00:44:01.460 --> 00:44:05.380
 I could keep you here all afternoon if I started listing those, so I won't.

00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:07.680
 But let me end by saying this.

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:17.000
 Everything we do, everything many of us do, inspires students to greatness, motivates them to strive for excellence,

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:23.780
 and reminds them of their responsibility to make a positive impact on the world.

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:24.820
 Just a small goal.

00:44:25.820 --> 00:44:33.280
 So, thank you, Carol, Don, Ben, Lester, for making this happen.

00:44:33.460 --> 00:44:38.180
 And a special thank you for Sheena Ramsey, who put this event together.

00:44:38.180 --> 00:44:38.320
 Thank you.

00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:51.000
 We owe you a bottle of champagne, and it is coming your way.

00:44:51.940 --> 00:45:01.340
 But please stay in touch with the center as we look forward to another year of engaging and inspiring the next generation of journalists.

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:02.000
 Thank you.
